; datom/store.bl — the STORAGE PORT: the only thing that knows a substrate.
;
; ── Why this file is the most important boundary in the system ─────────
;
; Everything above it (datoms, indexes, transactions, datalog) is pure
; logic over an ordered key/value space. Everything below it is somebody
; else's storage engine. This protocol is the seam, and it is deliberately
; TINY — five operations — because every method here is a method that a
; future substrate must implement, and a fat port is a port nothing else
; can ever fill.
;
; The plan (PLAN-033) targets Hobbes as the eventual substrate: a
; FoundationDB-architecture distributed store, in Elixir, with strictly
; serializable transactions over an ordered keyspace. Hobbes is pre-alpha
; and has NO PUBLIC API yet, so its shape WILL churn. That risk is
; contained precisely here: `store-ets` is the substrate the database is
; developed and tested against, `store-hobbes` arrives later behind the
; same five methods, and no layer above ever learns which one it has.
;
; ── Why ORDERED matters (the one non-obvious requirement) ─────────────
;
; A hash store answers "what is at key K?". An EAV index needs "every key
; between K1 and K2, in order" — because a covering index IS an
; order-preserving key encoding plus a range scan over it. Pattern
; `[?e :person/age ?a]` becomes a scan of the AEVT range prefixed by
; :person/age. Without ordered range scans there is no index, only a full
; table scan wearing a costume.
;
; ∴ `-range` is the load-bearing method. `-get` is a convenience that
; could be expressed as a one-element range; it exists because point
; lookups are frequent enough to deserve a direct path.

(ns datom.store)

(defprotocol Store
  "An ordered, transactional key/value space.

   Keys are byte-comparable encoded terms (see datom/codec.bl): the
   ordering of the ENCODED key is what indexes rely on, so a substrate
   must compare keys as opaque sortable values and never reinterpret
   them.

   Implementations must satisfy the conformance suite in
   test/bl/datom/store_test.bl — that suite is the actual contract, and
   it runs unchanged against every substrate."

  (-get [store k]
    "The value at `k`, or nil when absent.

     nil is unambiguous here because the layers above never STORE nil: a
     retracted datom is represented by absence, and an index entry's
     value is a non-nil marker. Substrates therefore need not distinguish
     'absent' from 'present but nil'.")

  (-range [store start stop]
    "All `[k v]` entries with `start <= k <= stop`, in ascending key
     order. `nil` on either bound means unbounded on that side.

     Bounds are INCLUSIVE — the same convention as `subseq` in core, so
     the two compose without an off-by-one translation layer at the seam.

     The result must be ordered. A substrate that cannot guarantee order
     cannot back an index and must not implement this protocol.")

  (-put [store k v]
    "Store `v` at `k`, replacing any existing value. Returns the store.

     Stores are VALUES to the layers above where the substrate allows it
     (the ETS impl returns a handle; a persistent impl may return a new
     store). Callers must always use the RETURN value, never assume
     mutation in place — that discipline is what lets a persistent
     substrate drop in without rewriting callers.")

  (-delete [store k]
    "Remove `k` (idempotent). Returns the store.")

  (-update [store k f]
    "ATOMICALLY replace the value at `k` with `(f current)`, where
     `current` is nil when absent. Returns `[store new-value]`.

     The sixth method, and the one that is NOT expressible in terms of
     the others. `-get` then `-put` is a read-modify-write RACE: two
     writers both read `t=41`, both write `t=42`, and the database has
     two transactions claiming one basis. Datomic avoids this with a
     single transactor; on the BEAM the natural equivalent is a
     single-writer process per database, but that is a policy the LAYER
     ABOVE chooses — the port still has to offer an atomic primitive so
     a substrate that HAS one (Hobbes: strict serializability) can supply
     it rather than being forced through a lock it does not need.

     Wave 2 needs this for the `:db.part/tx` counter; wave 4 needs it for
     `:db/unique` upsert, where 'does this value already exist?' and
     'claim it' must be one indivisible step.

     `f` must be PURE and may be retried by a substrate that implements
     this as an optimistic CAS loop.")

  (-commit [store ops]
    "Apply a batch of operations ATOMICALLY. Returns the store.

     `ops` is a sequence of `[:put k v]` and `[:delete k]` vectors.
     Either every operation is visible or none is — a transaction that
     writes four index entries for one datom must never be observable
     with two of them written.

     This is the method that makes the EAV layer's transactions real, and
     it is why the port targets a TRANSACTIONAL substrate rather than a
     plain KV store. An implementation that cannot offer atomicity (a
     bare file, say) must say so loudly rather than silently degrading —
     a torn index is a corrupt database, not a slow one."))

(defn put-all
  "Apply `[k v]` entries to `store` atomically via `-commit`.

   A convenience over the protocol, not a sixth method: it lowers to the
   same `-commit` every substrate already implements."
  [store entries]
  (-commit store (mapv (fn [e] [:put (get e 0) (get e 1)]) entries)))

(defn range-keys
  "Just the keys of `(-range store start stop)`, in order."
  [store start stop]
  (mapv (fn [e] (get e 0)) (-range store start stop)))

(defn next-id
  "Atomically increment the counter at `k` and return the new value.

   The canonical `-update` use: entity- and transaction-id allocation.
   Starts at 1 when the counter does not yet exist."
  [store k]
  (get (-update store k (fn [current] (if (nil? current) 1 (inc current)))) 1))

(defn range-count
  "How many entries lie between the bounds.

   Deliberately NOT a protocol method: a substrate with a cheap counting
   primitive can be given one later, but requiring it up front would make
   the port harder to fill for no present gain."
  [store start stop]
  (count (-range store start stop)))
